{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013xWESP5vShUpu4KHYLrho5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: Gauntlet\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## Goal\n33\t\n34\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n35\t\n36\tScope\n37\t\n38\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n39\t\n40\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n41\t\n42\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n43\t\n44\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n45\t\n46\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n47\t\n48\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n49\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n50\t\n51\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n52\t\n53\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n54\t\n55\t## Done\n56\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n57\t\n58\t## Source Information\n59\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n60\t- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:50\n61\t- Source: the work it's based on\n62\t- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n63\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n64\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n65\t- Art Direction: style used\n66\t- Animation: system used\n67\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n68\t- Online: network features used, or none\n69\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n70\t- Research: research done, or none\n71\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n72\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n73\t- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n74\t\n75\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n76\t{\n77\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n78\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n79\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n80\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n81\t  \"aiHub\": {\n82\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n83\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n84\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n85\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n86\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n87\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n88\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n89\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n90\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n91\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n92\t  }\n93\t}\n94\t\n95\tTesting Feedback\n96\t\n97\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n98\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n99\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n100\t- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n101\t\n102\tEngine Feedback\n103\t\n104\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n105\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n106\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n107\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n108\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n109\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n110\t\n111\tPrompt Feedback \n112\t\n113\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n114\t- What was confusing?\n115\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n116\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"04adf679-869d-4bcd-bcf3-495063b939db","uuid":"fd15b0f0-488e-4f1a-b02f-bee4072107fe","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:12:28.364Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050250__x8ffc89/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: Gauntlet\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:50\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback \n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","numLines":117,"startLine":1,"totalLines":117}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013xWESP5vShUpu4KHYLrho5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwpGHMaeyKmUaw8xBwh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now read `TASK.md` in one complete pass (all 117 lines). One part of the file I hadn't covered before is the \"Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens\" rule. The dev panel is paged and big-type, and the front-end screens each use one idea per page, so that holds. Each requirement also matches what I built and ran earlier, including the title RNG pick, the dev mode, the attract mode, the Xbox glyph option and the touch-only notice. The bundled `Gauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050250.html` and a parsing, fully filled `exit-interview.json` are in the run folder's root.\n\nNot verified, because it can't be done in this environment:\n- **Audio:** I cannot listen, so the sound effects, songs and voices were never heard.\n- **Hardware:** I did not use a physical DualSense or a real touch device. The controller was a mocked standard-mapping pad, and the touch-only notice was tested by forcing the browser to report no fine pointer.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:50\n- **Source:** Gauntlet (the arcade co-op dungeon crawler)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade co-op dungeon crawler (twin-stick hack-and-shoot with maze exploration), three-quarter overhead camera (top-down and isometric selectable).\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (unmodified), plain JavaScript built from 14 source fragments, AI Hub client session.js 1.4.0 (unmodified). Playwright 1.63.0 and Node 24 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel-art style with 3D-skeleton characters, three dungeon realms plus the Banquet, torchlight. New art in code: item and HUD sprites, generator art, projectiles, title scene.\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid and Blob rigs and combat moves, plus telegraphed enemy wind-ups and monsters that shrink as they lose rank.\n- **Audio:** all in code, no audio files: about 60 sound effects, 7 songs, and Animalese-style gibberish voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1-4 players (passes the hub harness in all four scenarios). Offline: solo and couch co-op.\n- **Testing:** `node tests/run.mjs` prints only failures and a count (1037 checks), including a scripted playthrough of all 11 stages. Plus the AI Hub harness scenarios, an Xbox-glyph scan of every screen, co-op join and drop-out in a browser, an 84-floor bot sweep (0 stalls), and mocked pad, mouse and touch emulation.\n- **Research:** read the engine handoff, the AI Hub README, harness and arena example, and the plan; nothing else.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; Chromium via Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About thirteen real ones:\n  - the boss hall's rows were different widths;\n  - the attract mode crashed the camera bounds when no floor was loaded;\n  - bots stuck on loot behind sealed walls, and again on loot they couldn't pick up;\n  - bots stunning each other at the boss;\n  - the Dev Tools' opening Enter press toggled a cheat at once;\n  - the first floor card showed the wrong realm line;\n  - HUD icons overlapped the health number and the corner buttons;\n  - pressing stick-right switched a joining player's class;\n  - high scores did not save where storage is blocked;\n  - a dropped-out player instantly rejoined;\n  - hard-coded PlayStation button names in a few prompts;\n  - a class-select label overlapped the quip text.\n\n  Two more failures were mistakes in my own test script.\n- **How many were game breaking?** Two: the unequal boss-hall rows (the last floor would not have built correctly) and the attract-mode camera crash (an error every frame).\n- **Most helpful test?** The scripted bot playthrough through the real scenes, plus the multi-seed floor sweep. They found stalls and the friendly-fire lock at the boss that no unit test would have.\n- **Least helpful test?** Static screenshots of menu screens. They confirmed layout but said nothing about feel, input or sound.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the Humanoid and Blob rigs with live portraits, canvas lighting, props, `cam.frame` for several heroes, `allowJoin`, and headless `game.advance`. The standard pause, settings and controls screens saved a lot of work.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** the pause menu's custom items don't close the menu, so Dev Tools opened behind it until I added `game.resume()`, and the confirm press then leaked into my Dev screen. A few docs left units and return shapes unclear (`cornerArea`, `mouseGround`, `toGround`). The Humanoid weapon list has no axe, bow or shield.\n- **Most important thing I built:** the simulation and everything around it: a multi-source flow field, monster AI with telegraphs, generators, ricochet and pierce shots, the seeded floor generator with its solver, the bot, Dev Tools, voice babble, high scores and the AI Hub shared-world layer.\n- **Hardest part:** the per-player input split for a custom preset, and finding the one true line in a very large API index.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** none.\n- **One change I would make:** a multi-source `FlowField` and a documented way to add custom Humanoid weapons (axe, bow, shield).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the plan's exact tables for heroes, monsters, items, tuning and messages, and the hub README with its harness giving a clear pass or fail.\n- **Confusing:** how much to build. The plan is 120 KB and written for a much bigger effort than medium, with engine changes (E1–E8) and hand-authored floors I chose not to follow.\n- **Hardest thing:** holding the whole scope together (eleven stages, couch co-op, online, attract mode, Dev Tools, the playthrough test, the feedback text) with nothing stubbed. The shared-world networking was the largest single piece.\n- **What I'd change:** give a priority order for the plan's sections when effort is limited, say whether the engine changes are required, and split the plan into must-haves and nice-to-haves."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3567,"cache_read_input_tokens":458674,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3567},"output_tokens":785,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"04adf679-869d-4bcd-bcf3-495063b939db","uuid":"71be0893-bc3f-4fb9-a5d6-c9b400f9f62d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:12:39.916Z","request_id":"req_011CghwpFwmoixJpmMTPPHXJ"}
